{
  "hostname": "BL-LT-0106",
  "reason": "Check disk space and warnings for file-save failures"
}

{
  "outcome": "needs_user_info",
  "reply_to_user": "Hi Leo, sorry this has dragged on, and thanks for sticking with it. I checked your laptop and found the cause: the drive is almost full. Only about 9 GB of 256 GB is free (3.5%), and that's why files won't save. Here's how to free up space:\n1. Empty the Recycle Bin and clear out your Downloads folder.\n2. Open Settings > System > Storage > Temporary files. Select temporary files and old Windows Update files, then remove them.\n3. In OneDrive, turn on Files On-Demand (OneDrive settings > Sync and back up > Advanced settings). Your synced files will then stay in the cloud until you open them.\n4. Uninstall any apps you no longer use (Settings > Apps > Installed apps).\nOnce you've done this, reply here and I'll check the drive again. If there still isn't enough free space, I'll pass this straight to our second-level team to look at a larger drive or a replacement laptop.",
  "internal_note": "Repeat request, so the user is frustrated. Device BL-LT-0106 (Win 11, online): disk 256 GB with 9 GB free (3.5%), plus a low disk space warning. That's well under KB-017's 10% threshold and matches the file-save failures. Sent the user the KB-017 cleanup steps (Recycle Bin/Downloads, temp files, OneDrive Files On-Demand, uninstall unused apps). Next: re-run check_device_status once the user confirms. Per KB-017, escalate to Tier 2 if still under 10% free (larger drive or replacement). Given the repeat contacts, a technician may want to assist directly or remotely.",
  "kb_articles": [
    "KB-017"
  ],
  "confidence": "high"
}
